@startuml finance-event-sourcing-flow-diagram

title Vankoo Finance: Event Sourcing Flow Diagram

skinparam shadowing false
skinparam ArrowColor #212121
skinparam rectangle {
    BorderColor #212121
    BackgroundColor #FFFFFF
}
skinparam database {
    BorderColor #212121
    BackgroundColor #FFFFFF
}
skinparam queue {
    BorderColor #212121
    BackgroundColor #FFFFFF
}
skinparam actor {
    BorderColor #212121
    BackgroundColor #FFFFFF
}

' =====================================================================
' USE CASE 1 - Creation: no replay, the aggregate is born from a command
' =====================================================================
rectangle "Use case 1 — Initiate deposit (creation)" as uc1 #FAFAFA {
    actor "Investor" as investor
    rectangle "Deposit REST\nController" as api1
    rectangle "Deposit Command\nService" as svc1
    rectangle "New Deposit\nAggregate" as agg1
    rectangle "DepositInitiatedEvent" as ev1 #FFF9C4
}

' =====================================================================
' USE CASE 2 - Modification: retrieve, replay, decide, produce
' =====================================================================
rectangle "Use case 2 — Apply provider update (modification)" as uc2 #FAFAFA {
    actor "Stripe" as stripe
    rectangle "Webhook Inbox\nService" as inbox2
    rectangle "Deposit Command\nService" as svc2
    rectangle "Deposit\nAggregate" as agg2
    rectangle "DepositSucceededEvent" as ev2 #FFF9C4
}

database "Axon Server\nEvent Store" as store
queue "Event Processors\n(projection + publisher)" as processors

' keep the reading order: use case 1 above use case 2
uc1 -[hidden]down-> uc2

' ---------- use case 1 ----------
investor --> api1 : POST /api/v1/deposits
api1 --> svc1 : InitiateDepositCommand
svc1 --> agg1 : creates
agg1 --> ev1 : produces
ev1 --> store : stored

' ---------- use case 2 ----------
stripe --> inbox2 : webhook
inbox2 --> svc2 : ApplyProviderDepositUpdateCommand
svc2 --> store : retrieves events\nfor depositId
store --> agg2 : replays events
agg2 --> agg2 : updates state
agg2 --> ev2 : produces
ev2 --> store : stored

' ---------- shared outcome ----------
store --> processors : published

note bottom of store
  <b>Event store after both operations</b>

  |= AGGREGATE_ID |= SEQ |= EVENT |
  | 2b7f1f7d… | 0 | DEPOSIT_INITIATED |
  | 2b7f1f7d… | 1 | DEPOSIT_PROVIDER_REFERENCE_REGISTERED |
  | 2b7f1f7d… | 2 | DEPOSIT_SUCCEEDED |

  Append-only. The aggregate itself is never written:
  there is no row holding the deposit state.
end note

legend bottom
  <b>The difference between the two use cases is the replay.</b>
  Creating a deposit needs no history: the command handler is a static factory.
  Changing one does: the aggregate is rebuilt from its events before it can decide,
  and only then produces the new event.

  <b>Only the event is persisted, never the aggregate.</b> Current state is a
  function of the event history, so it can always be recomputed and never
  disagrees with the log.

  The read model in PostgreSQL is built by the projection downstream of
  "published", and can be dropped and rebuilt from these same events.
endlegend

@enduml
